\begin{abstract}
We describe clawmarc, a catalog card for digital artefacts that is exactly
4096 bytes long, binds by SHA-256 to the bytes it describes, carries a short
human-readable description alongside a machine-readable search payload,
records retrieval hints for several storage backends, and carries an Ed25519
signature. The card is a descriptor, not a proof of authenticity or
availability, and it is meant to live separate from the artefact; its
artefact classes are the twelve terms of the DCMI Type Vocabulary, and subject
description is left to the schemes a cataloger already uses. We give the
design and its rationale, state the trust model under which a signature means
anything, place the card against MARC, Dublin Core, Linked Data, WARC,
Memento, and BagIt, and report what has been built: a specification with a C
reference layout, a Python producer and inspector, reference cards, cards
that pin the exact embedding models the format names, a differential test in
which two independent implementations produced byte-identical cards on every
pinned field, and retrieval experiments on a judged collection and a
topic-proxy collection. The experiments do not propose a retrieval method;
they ask whether a fixed card can carry representations from models with
materially different behaviour while keeping one catalog interface. The
contribution claimed is a substrate, and its costs and open questions are
stated with it.
\end{abstract}

